# ============================================================================
#  Dendra — testnet miner config.  Copy this file to  .env  then fill it in.
#      cp .env.example .env
# ============================================================================

# --- PUBLIC testnet endpoints (provided by the network operator) ---
#  Ask the operator for them, or get them from the testnet page.

# CHAIN RPC — where this miner reads the chain and sends its transactions.
#
#  RECOMMENDED, and what `deploy/join.sh` sets up on its own: run YOUR OWN node next to the miner. The
#  miner then reads it on the node kit's `dendra-chain` Docker network, by the node's compose project
#  name (`dendra-node` unless that kit sets DENDRA_PROJECT), and the node's RPC stays published on
#  127.0.0.1 only. Two lines, written for you by `join.sh` after it starts the node and waits for it:
#      DENDRA_NODE=tcp://dendra-node:26657
#      COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.local-node.yml
#  COMPOSE_FILE adds docker-compose.local-node.yml, which puts the miner on that network. Setting it stops
#  compose from reading docker-compose.override.yml by itself: when that GPU file exists, append
#  :docker-compose.override.yml to the line. Run compose from THIS directory without -f, or the line is
#  not read. (An older kit named tcp://host.docker.internal:26657 here: that only reaches a node whose RPC
#  is published on every interface, which the node kit no longer does by default.)
#
#  Why it is worth the disk it costs: a miner reading someone else's RPC has to TRUST what it is told
#  about the chain, goes blind the moment that endpoint stops, and can be shown a filtered view. You
#  already keep this machine running — it is the natural place for a node.
#
#  The address below is the fallback: the operator's public RPC (`join.sh --remote-rpc`). It works, and
#  it makes this miner depend on one machine it does not control.
DENDRA_NODE=tcp://RPC_PUBLIC_FILL_ME:26657
DENDRA_RELAY=http://RELAY_PUBLIC_FILL_ME:8645
FAUCET=http://FAUCET_PUBLIC_FILL_ME:4500

# NETWORK-INFO -- the address of the network's settings file, the CONFIG_URL given to deploy/join.sh (which
#  writes it here). deploy/testnet-miner/miner_health.sh reads that file again to compare this kit with what
#  the network publishes (KIT_VERSION, CONSENSUS_EPOCH, its public RPC). Not passed to the container. Empty,
#  those comparisons are reported as not measured.
CONFIG_URL=

# --- RELAY TOKEN: LEAVE IT EMPTY. You do not need one to mine. ---
#  A network that calls itself permissionless cannot be entered through a secret handed out by hand,
#  so nothing on the path to receiving work is gated on this. `GET list` — the work queue — is open,
#  because what a secret in front of it would hide (which job is assigned to which miner) is already
#  published by the chain to anyone who asks. A miner authenticates by SIGNING what it writes; the
#  kit derives it from the identity the miner actually resolved on-chain, at each publication.
#  Set this ONLY if you operate the relay yourself, or drive the gateway's `POST req/…` route.
DENDRA_RELAY_TOKEN=

# --- FAUCET PROOF OF WORK (public network) ---
#  A public faucet requires a proof of work bound to your address. The miner reads the required
#  difficulty from the faucet itself and solves it at startup — nothing to fill in here. Raise the
#  solving time limit only if the operator picked a very high difficulty (default 300 s).
# DENDRA_FAUCET_POW_MAX_S=300

# --- PINNED model served by the network (must match the on-chain registry) ---
#  This is THE model that will be downloaded LOCALLY on your machine and served by your GPU.
DENDRA_MODEL_ID=llama3.1:8b-instruct-q4_K_M

# --- VERIFICATION embedder: REAL SEMANTIC (not the lexical bag-of-words) ---
#  On-chain verification compares embeddings. In "backend" mode, the miner embeds via Ollama (/api/embeddings)
#  with a DEDICATED embedding model -> we measure MEANING, not just shared words. All miners in a
#  committee must use the SAME one (keep these values).
DENDRA_EMBED_MODE=backend
DENDRA_EMBED_API_MODEL=nomic-embed-text

# --- YOUR miner's identity (unique on the network; keep it stable) ---
MINER_ID=m-change-me

# --- THE KEYRING'S PASSPHRASE: a DIRECTORY, mounted read-only into the miner at /run/dendra-secrets ---
#  It holds one file, keyring-passphrase (the directory 0700, the file 0600), OUTSIDE the miner-keys volume
#  and outside this clone. deploy/join.sh creates it for a new installation and writes this line; for an
#  installation whose keys are still in clear, deploy/testnet-miner/encrypt-keys.sh does (plan first, --yes
#  to encrypt). Whether the keys are encrypted is read from the volume itself, never from this file: an
#  encrypted keyring whose passphrase is missing stops the miner with the reason, it never makes a new key.
#  Empty or absent: an empty ./secrets is mounted, and a miner with keys in clear runs as before.
#  BACK UP BOTH the miner-keys volume and this file: without the passphrase the encrypted keyring is lost,
#  and with it the account, unless its 24-word recovery phrase was written down. What this protects: the
#  volume and every backup of it. What it does not: a host compromised while the miner runs.
#  The passphrase is NEVER set here as a variable (DENDRA_MINER_PASSPHRASE is not forwarded to the miner:
#  `docker inspect` would print it).
#  A DIRECTORY OF ITS OWN: the kit sets it 0700, backs it up, and uninstall --delete-keys removes the
#  passphrase from it -- so join.sh, encrypt-keys.sh and uninstall.sh refuse $HOME, a directory above it, the
#  application's ~/.config/dendra, and one that already holds other files (passphrase-dir.sh, next to this file).
# DENDRA_SECRETS_DIR=/home/you/.config/dendra/miner-secrets

# --- FINAL TESTNET SEASON PAYOUT ADDRESS (optional, strongly advised) ---
#  Without it the season pays this machine's own key, which anyone who copies this machine's keys can
#  spend. Use an address whose key is NOT on this machine. deploy/join.sh --payout-address dendra1...
#  checks its checksum and writes this line; the miner declares it once it is registered, and again only
#  when it changes.
# DENDRA_PAYOUT_ADDRESS=

# --- OWNER MODE (optional, advanced: a second device holding a key, with dendrad from the release) ---
#  The address of the key that REGISTERS this miner and holds its stake, kept off this machine. The
#  miner's identifier is derived from it; the miner never sends create-miner itself: it prints the three
#  commands (prepare here, sign offline on the owner's device, broadcast here) and mines once the chain
#  records this machine's key as the operator. Only the owner can delete or update the miner, and the
#  stake goes back to the owner. What it protects: the STAKE. What it does not: the income -- payments,
#  the subsidy and the season's default payout go to the operator, this machine's key -- nor the stake
#  against a slash caused by bad commits signed with a copy of this machine's key. One owner address
#  registers ONE miner. deploy/join.sh --owner dendra1... checks the checksum and writes this line. It
#  applies to a NEW identity: a miner already registered by this machine keeps working without it.
# DENDRA_MINER_OWNER=

# --- AUDIT COMMITTEE (optional; needs a second, CPU-only Ollama -- start with `--profile judge`) ---
#  DENDRA_JUDGE_MODEL_ID  is the model DECLARED in your on-chain verdicts, and the FALLBACK used when
#                         the chain pins none. It does NOT beat the on-chain pin.
#  DENDRA_JUDGE_MODEL_OVERRIDE forces the model actually run by the judge (highest precedence).
#  Precedence: OVERRIDE > on-chain pin (modelregistry.audit_judge_model) > _MODEL_ID > built-in default.
#  Never qwen3:4b as a judge: it false-slashes honest miners in a distributed setting.
# DENDRA_MINER_JUDGE=1
# DENDRA_JUDGE_ENDPOINT=http://ollama-cpu:11434
# DENDRA_JUDGE_MODEL_ID=qwen3:30b-a3b-instruct-2507-q4_K_M
# DENDRA_JUDGE_MODEL_OVERRIDE=
#  COMPOSE_PROFILES=judge makes a plain `docker compose up -d` from this directory start the judge's
#  CPU instance too (compose reads it here, like COMPOSE_FILE); without it a restart leaves it stopped.
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=judge

# Image the miner service starts. Leave it unset: join.sh writes it itself, and only from the clone's
# docker/MINER_IMAGE (a digest-pinned image of the official package, committed after a release); with
# no pin there, when the pull fails, or when the pulled image's platform is not that of the Docker
# engine, the kit builds dendra/miner:latest from this tree. A value served by network-info.txt is
# ignored: that file travels over plain HTTP. Set it by hand only for a compose that pulls an image
# built elsewhere from the same tree, and start it only on an engine of the image's platform -- the two
# commands below print the same value:
#   docker compose pull miner
#   docker image inspect --format '{{.Os}}/{{.Architecture}}' "$DENDRA_MINER_IMAGE"
#   docker version --format '{{.Server.Os}}/{{.Server.Arch}}'
#   docker compose up -d --no-build
# DENDRA_MINER_IMAGE=
